Flicker-Chantry
Ingredients: SINGER/SONGWRITER + POWER METAL + HAUNTING, SPOOKY & GHOSTLY + Guitar + 2020s ERA
Creative Catalyst: Born in damp maritime coastal projection booths where rogue projectionists spun flickering blank celluloid, improvising manic double-kick guitar hymns and desolate confessional lyrics to accompany phantom, unmade silent horror films.

Flicker-Chantry is an explosive, uncanny collision of weeping acoustic confessionals and hyper-speed, widescreen power metal majesty. Intimate, close-mic'd nylon-string murmurs and trembling baritone narratives are suddenly pulverized by 190 BPM galloping double-kick percussion, blistering sweep-picked neoclassical guitar duels, and banshee-like four-octave operatic choruses. Swathed in tape hiss, physical 35mm projector-blade flutter, and cavernous plate reverbs, the music evokes an antique ghost ship caught in a storm of virtuoso theatricality.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 140–190 BPM; radical, disorienting metric modulations that pivot instantly from rubato acoustic folk fingerpicking to relentless, galloping sixteenth-note power metal battery.
- Harmonic Palette: Aeolian and Harmonic Minor foundations shot through with Phrygian dominant flourishes, dramatic Picardy thirds, and sweeping, operatic truck-driver key shifts drenched in gothic melodrama.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. The Nitrated Vocal Chain: Hyper-dry, ASMR-intimate whispered vocals abruptly mirrored and multiplied into colossal 40-voice operatic choir stacks treated with pitch-decaying tape flutter.
  2. Sprocket-Delay Guitars: Scalloped-fret electric guitar sweeps and folk acoustics routed through modified 16mm magnetic film playback heads to generate mechanical flutter, spectral print-through, and micro-dropouts.
  3. Shutter-Gating: Harsh, rhythmic digital choke-cuts applied to distorted rhythm guitars, mimicking the physical light-shutter chopping of an antique projector running at 24 frames per second.
- Dynamics: A typical composition starts in shivering, candlelit silence—a cracked voice accompanied by a single creaking acoustic guitar—before a solitary scream triggers a blinding, symphonic blastbeat onslaught, eventually collapsing back into the lonely mechanical whir of an empty film spindle.

Scene & Origin Lore
Flicker-Chantry emerged during the winter fog of the early 2020s along the decaying pier-cinemas of Aberdeen, Scotland, and the storm-battered coast of the Pacific Northwest. Disillusioned projectionists, tasked with running automated multiplex systems while historic movie palaces sat condemned, broke into abandoned projection rooms to project completely blank, damaged reels of acetate onto salt-crusted silver screens. Staring into blinding rectangles of dust motes and film-burn, they plugged acoustic dreadnoughts and 8-string guitars into communal amplifier stacks, wildly improvising live scores to nonexistent German Expressionist and maritime disaster epics they claimed to see in the grain. Their subculture, known as "The Shutter-Priesthood," gathers for clandestine midnight vigils in condemned seaside theaters wearing salt-stained tweed overcoats pinned with brass film spools and iron sprockets. Performances begin with communal, whispered acoustic confessions before disintegrating into circle pits illuminated only by spinning carbon-arc projector beams.

Typical Instruments
- Guitars: Scalloped-fret 8-string electric guitars, vintage acoustic dreadnoughts with internal contact mics, nylon-string classical guitars played with metal thumbpicks, and cello-bowed guitars.
- Synthesizers & Keys: Sampled 19th-century church pipe organs, harmoniums, and granular film-flutter Mellotrons.
- Percussion & Bass: Dual-kick drum kits muffled with heavy maritime wool blankets, orchestral timpani, and rumbling sub-octave fretless electric bass.
- Mechanical Oddities: Hand-cranked 35mm cinema projectors mic’d with contact transducers to act as mechanical rhythmic metronomes.

